<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Plauen</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Plauen is a city in the Vogtland region of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-elster">White Elster</a>. From the 13th century it was the seat of a branch of the advocates (Vögte) of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weida">Weida</a>, and it later passed to Saxony. In the 19th century it became famous for machine-made Plauen lace, and in October 1989 it saw one of the first mass demonstrations of the peaceful revolution in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">East Germany</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/plauen.jpg" alt="Plauen" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tilman2007 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plauen,_Altes_und_Neues_Rathaus,_004.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From about 1209 a branch ruled <b>Plauen</b>, and the whole region came to be called the Vogtland after their title.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-plauen">Lordship of Plauen</a></li><li><blockquote>The Reuss lords descended from the Vögte (advocates) of Weida, Gera and <b>Plauen</b>, who ruled the Vogtland from the 12th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-reuss-gera">Principality of Reuss-Gera</a></li><li><blockquote>The counts, descended from the medieval Vögte of Weida, Gera and <b>Plauen</b>, held small territories in eastern Thuringia.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-reuss-schleiz">Principality of Reuss-Schleiz</a></li></ul></section>
